We are releasing n outputs adaptively, where each mechanism Mi only accesses its own disjoint subset Xi of the data, and the partition is data-independent. Differential privacy under disjointness and data-independent partition enjoys parallel composition: if each Mi is (εi, δi)-DP with respect to changes in its own Xi, then the joint release over the union X is (maxi εi, maxi δi)-DP. Intuitively, a single-record change can affect at most one subset Xi, hence at most one mechanism’s output distribution; the others are unchanged. Even though later mechanisms may depend on earlier releases (adaptive/interactive composition), they still receive no additional information about records outside their designated subset except what is already DP-protected by previous outputs. Therefore, the privacy loss is governed by the worst per-part mechanism, not the sum.

Formally, for neighboring datasets X and X′ that differ in one individual located in subset Xk, the joint distribution of outputs factorizes so that only the kth component’s conditional distribution can differ, while all others (conditioned on previous outputs) remain identical across X and X′. Since Mk is (εk, δk)-DP with respect to Xk even under arbitrary side information (including prior outputs), the likelihood ratio of the full transcript is bounded by e^{εk} except with probability δk. Taking the worst k yields (maxi εi, maxi δi)-DP.

\boxed{yes}
